  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cooler airmass continues to build into the area\nthis morning behind yesterday's cold front as high pressure\nexpands in from the west. Today will be the coolest day of the\nweek until next weekend (see the next key message) with highs in\nthe low to mid 60s. With periods of light winds tonight, likely\ninterrupted by a reinforcing front passing over, some patchy\nfrost is possible overnight in well sheltered areas where lows\nmay fall into the upper 30s. This may be tempered by a thick\ndeck of cirri passing overhead. \n\nMonday night continues to look like the best candidate for more\nwidespread frost as skies remain clear and winds inland will\nlikely be calm as the high crests overhead. Like the previous\nshift, nudged overnight lows down a few degrees closer to NBM\n10th percentile, and did the same for Tuesday night with little\nchange in the pattern, resulting in forecast lows in the mid to\nupper 30s.\n\nBeyond this period, guidance continues to depict an \nincreasingly unsettled pattern as complex mid-level low deepens \nover the eastern CONUS and likely drives coastal low development\noff the east coast. Evolution and strength of the low remain\nuncertain, but it will at least bring our best chances of rain \nfor the period and breezier conditions across the Outer Banks.\nStay tuned for more details in the coming days.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Guidance continues to strongly hint at \nunseasonable cold temperatures next weekend behind the low \npressure system. While there is inherent uncertainty 7 days out,\nCPC does have a slight risk of much below normal temperatures \nfor our coastal plain counties next weekend. This will be worth \nmonitoring for any freeze headlines, as the end of the growing \nseason is Nov 25 for our land forecast zones.",
